{"canonical_record":{"canonical_payload_sha256":"7280cc91bd1491c8141412f980b3f0ff89597e488b3ae882c30e18a5152d3d69","full_review_record_id":"fullrev_05097111ccc0070a5f16ed5cf1e2ef889f609238451896c7fe67a1c8e88ef019","overall_assessment":"The paper proposes a general framework for reference-aware language models that explicitly models the decision to generate tokens from external sources (databases, lists) or internal context (coreference). The work addresses a significant gap in neural language modeling by treating reference as a stochastic latent variable, which is a conceptually strong contribution. The empirical results demonstrate clear improvements in handling out-of-vocabulary and entity-specific tokens across three distinct tasks, validating the utility of the proposed architecture for tasks requiring precise entity grounding.","paper_id":"ByG8A7cee","paper_identity_sha256":"28492e9dd4c1727dde0f29dc7c47221af20018bc36248760cfa8ffe698aec7ca","prompt_identity_sha256":"ab105142349328796a60d9981f445e5cd1b9667c0e3f8f197065d4ec6839b12e","raw_response_sha256":"4b4da87a15260a67b33bfab4d9bf3df009367bd58a6199ebeb7c9ca7db2e26dd","request_identity_sha256":"8663e04eb620166d04c0b38838592b1e89febcbcb81430f21c66171a3746d6e7","reviewer_identity_sha256":"c1cd7c7fdd4a2cbeb45c2f21af982ad0f30bffae8ae148e3eba36479685cf360","role":"SIGN","schema_version":"agentreview-full-review-corpus-v4","structural_context_sha256":"432180d6af9c9c42747cc57ad50fb4ca684b723dc4197de60e5007aa04764c08","subdimension_sections":[{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-001","SRC-002","SRC-004"],"review_text":"The work addresses a broadly important problem in natural language processing: the explicit modeling of reference decisions in language generation. By treating reference as a stochastic latent variable rather than a deterministic attention mechanism, the paper introduces a paradigm that can influence multiple research areas, including dialogue systems, text generation, and coreference resolution. The framework's generality, allowing it to handle external databases, item lists, and internal discourse context, suggests a wide potential for adoption by researchers working on entity-grounded generation tasks.","subdimension_id":"SIGN-BRD"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-003","SRC-020","SRC-022"],"review_text":"The paper demonstrates domain-specific impact across three distinct application areas: task-oriented dialogue (restaurant recommendation), recipe generation, and coreference-based language modeling. In the dialogue domain, the model effectively queries a database to generate accurate responses, addressing a key challenge in task-oriented systems. For recipe generation, it shows improved handling of ingredient lists. In coreference, it improves entity perplexity. These results indicate that the method is not limited to a single niche but offers practical benefits in specialized fields where precise entity reference is critical.","subdimension_id":"SIGN-DOM"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-027","SRC-028","SRC-029","SRC-030","SRC-031"],"review_text":"The paper reports substantial improvements over state-of-the-art baselines, particularly in handling out-of-vocabulary (OOV) and entity-specific tokens. In the dialogue task, the table pointer model achieves a significantly lower perplexity for table OOV tokens (82.86) compared to the seq2seq baseline (2.83E9), demonstrating a clear advantage in generating rare or unseen entities. Similarly, in the coreference task, the pointer model with initialization reduces entity perplexity from 43.86 to 28.56. These gains are consistent across tasks and metrics, indicating that the proposed method offers a meaningful improvement over existing attention-based approaches.","subdimension_id":"SIGN-SOT"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-034"],"review_text":"The paper does not explicitly discuss the broader societal or real-world impact of the proposed models, such as fairness, environmental impact, or potential harms. While the technical improvements in dialogue and text generation could have practical applications, the paper focuses primarily on perplexity and BLEU scores. The conclusion mentions future work on human evaluation to assess if the model can reply to users' queries accurately, but it does not address who benefits from these improvements or any potential risks associated with deploying such systems in real-world settings.","subdimension_id":"SIGN-IMP"}]},"disposition":"CANONICAL_FULL_REVIEW_COMMITTED","item_commit_sha256":"428011d77888ce328d67527e49d11f739239c761c1a1d9caaab4a4dab097d666","paper_id":"ByG8A7cee","paper_ordinal":69,"raw_attempts":[{"assistant_content_base64":"","assistant_content_sha256":"4b4da87a15260a67b33bfab4d9bf3df009367bd58a6199ebeb7c9ca7db2e26dd","attempt_index":0,"error":"","finish_reason":"stop","paper_id":"ByG8A7cee","raw_provider_response_base64":"","raw_provider_response_sha256":"e0007e73e95c88f7fe92510e12fbf6a5df76eed1be4f72a0232c0e601dab666a","request_identity_sha256":"8663e04eb620166d04c0b38838592b1e89febcbcb81430f21c66171a3746d6e7","role":"SIGN","schema_version":"no-memory-full-review-corpus-build-v5","usage":{"completion_tokens":833,"prompt_tokens":20931,"prompt_tokens_details":null,"total_tokens":21764},"valid":true}],"request_identity_sha256":"8663e04eb620166d04c0b38838592b1e89febcbcb81430f21c66171a3746d6e7","role":"SIGN","role_ordinal":2,"schema_version":"corpus-build-bootstrap-checkpoint-v4"}